---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "inhabited-worlds-2026-11-15",
);
const root = "/studies/inhabited-worlds-2026-11-15";
const concepts = [
  {
    id: "ocean",
    bodyFraction: 1158 / 1254,
    title: "A / Oceans with somewhere to live",
    question:
      "Can exposed geography and selective weather suggest life below without explicit civilization marks?",
    limit:
      "Familiar continents may carry Earth recognition while leaving habitation merely assumed.",
  },
  {
    id: "weather",
    bodyFraction: 1216 / 1254,
    title: "B / A world beneath weather",
    question:
      "Can cloud and atmospheric depth make the small body feel vast, with land glimpsed beneath it?",
    limit:
      "Weather may hide both recognizable geography and any sense of people, or approach Venus's cloud-covered reading.",
  },
  {
    id: "settled",
    bodyFraction: 1165 / 1254,
    title: "C / Life continuing after sunset",
    question:
      "Can a few organized settlement lights make habitation legible while the world keeps its mass and quiet areas?",
    limit:
      "Small lights may vanish at flight scale or become decorative sparkle; this view cannot establish their daylight counterpart.",
  },
];
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-label="Study outcome">
    <h2>Stopped · 15 November 2026</h2>
    <p>
      The designer stopped this study because it was looping and failing to
      resolve the central design question. No planet appearance, atmospheric
      mechanic or implementation direction was accepted. Stopping preserves
      useful observations; it does not make the design effort successful.
    </p>
    <p>
      Designer assessment: “we are looping” and “it seems like a model collapse
      on oversteering again.” Detailed planets did not belong to the game; the
      next drawings read as vector icons; the subsequent encounter example
      misused the Motion instrument and did not follow its specification,
      intended use or the design system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agent interpretation, not an established cause: corrections were
      repeatedly converted into new directions without a grounded account of the
      visual mismatch. The work moved from detailed imagery to vector drawing,
      then from promising cloud depth to an encounter apparatus. Narrowing
      changed the subject without resolving that gap. The conversation cannot
      establish an internal model-collapse mechanism.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retained findings: geography can support recognition without habitation;
      weather contributes a sense of life; small settlement lights need
      exaggeration to be legible; clouds appearing above ground showed promise.
      These observations accept neither a complete appearance nor a rendering
      method. Atmospheric danger beyond established gravity remains provisional.
    </p>
    <p>
      The retained 02.1 scenes are static evidence. Rejected 02.2–02.4 proposals
      and their dedicated code were removed. There is no accepted result to
      assess for implementation or size into delivery work. A separate
      diagnostic brief requests analysis only, not another study.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="01">01 / Life below, danger on approach</h2>
    <p>
      A duel passes above a world where life continues below. Beside the Sun,
      mineral Mercury, cloud-covered Venus and a small ship, Earth should feel
      like a vast inhabited place with an atmosphere. Its gravity makes approach
      consequential. The intended result is one Earth-like planet visual
      language demonstrated in a composed Lab scene, with recognizable Earth as
      its first prototype and room for fictional geography.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between suggesting that world in a small image and
      preserving the clarity of flight. Familiar land, oceans, weather,
      atmospheric light and traces of civilization are possible contributors;
      their prominence and necessity remain open. Simplification and
      exaggeration serve the whole impression. Earth recognition alone cannot
      establish a reusable language, and blue-green colour alone may suggest
      habitability without habitation. Those are hypotheses to examine, not
      prescribed features or diagnoses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hold the established art direction, flight controls, gravity law and
      solid-body contact motion fixed. Use the current Practice G7 Sun, Mercury,
      Venus and starter Wasp as scene context; comparison sizes, placement and
      mass are fixtures, not Earth tuning. Composition, material, light and
      visible motion remain open before choosing rendering methods. A narrow
      atmospheric heating zone is a possibility for a dangerous close pass, not
      an accepted mechanic: a visual sketch can explore its impression but
      cannot settle damage, drag or speed dependence. Those rules and production
      integration are adjacent work. Judge flight-scale scenes first;
      enlargements explain construction and do not settle the encounter.
    </p>
    <p>
      Last unresolved focus · 02.4 isolated the atmospheric encounter: what
      should a pilot see and understand while approaching, skimming and leaving?
      The broad visual-language question remains the study context, not a claim
      that this round will resolve the whole planet. Gravity and contact stay
      fixed; atmospheric extent and heating cues are provisional proposals.
    </p>
    <aside>
      <p>
        Question test · 15 November 2026: One subject — Pass; Grounded — Pass
        with findings; Consequence — Pass. The fixed contact motion and Practice
        comparison bodies were clarified after review; not retested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/inhabited-worlds-2026-11-15"
          >Full result and responses</a
        >.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="02">02 / What makes the encounter feel inhabited?</h2>
    <p>
      The first comparison varies the world's whole character: exposed
      geography, atmospheric depth, or visible settlement. All three occupy the
      same composed scene. These are alternative visual hypotheses, not a
      selection of features to combine. Weather now carries a useful sense of
      life; a complete appearance and production technique remain open.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Precedents / what their decisions establish</summary>
      <p>
        <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12#02-9">Mercury</a>
        recorded improvement from concrete composed surfaces after symbolic
        craters, hull-like planes and smoothing failed to establish material.
        Its later procedural contrast boost did not recover organized features;
        pleasing stills also failed to establish convincing orbital motion.
        “Suggest a world” was explicitly adopted into the design principles. The
        preferred mineral target guided subsequent surface synthesis.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/lab/studies/venus-2026-11-13#02-2">Venus</a>
        recorded a quiet body and distinct atmospheric presence as its direction
        after patterned cloud sketches read as gas giants. It retained faint
        structure, depth and amber colour while leaving the enlarged edge
        provisional. Softening the edge produced a button-like reading;
        returning to the composed scene advanced the whole-world question.
      </p>
      <p>
        Working interpretation: retain useful whole-world qualities without
        requiring every detail of their first example. Earth may need a
        different hierarchy. Neither precedent selects its palette, atmosphere,
        cities or construction method.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Fresh references / geography, atmosphere and settlement</summary>
      <div class="references">
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${root}/reference-blue.jpg`}
            alt="NASA Blue Marble composite with North America, dark oceans and organized white cloud systems."
            width="800"
            height="800"
          >
          <figcaption>
            <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/blue-marble-2002/"
              >Blue Marble, 2002</a
            >
            · NASA Earth Observatory. Connected land and dark ocean remain
            visible beneath large weather systems. This is a composite, not an
            instantaneous photograph;
            <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-blue-marble-true-color-global-imagery-at-1km-resolution/"
              >NASA documents its construction</a
            >. Design inference: choose which structures survive reduction
            rather than trying to retain all of its fine detail.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${root}/reference-night.jpg`}
            alt="NASA night Earth composite showing uneven concentrations of settlement light around a dark Atlantic."
            width="1041"
            height="586"
          >
          <figcaption>
            <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/black-marble/"
              >Black Marble, 2013</a
            >
            · NASA. The 2012 satellite observations are composited over Blue
            Marble imagery. Settlement has concentrations and dark gaps. Design
            inference: organized sparse light could suggest people; uniform
            glitter would lose that relationship. Visibility at game scale is
            unproven.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src={`${root}/reference-limb.jpg`}
            alt="Astronaut photograph showing clouds below a blue atmosphere fading upward into space, with the Moon visible behind it."
            width="540"
            height="334"
          >
          <figcaption>
            <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-top-of-the-atmosphere-7373/"
              >The Top of the Atmosphere</a
            >
            · NASA/JSC, ISS013-E-54329, 20 July 2006; NASA's crop and contrast
            enhancement. Air fades into space above the cloud layer. This close
            horizon view cannot prescribe the width of a tiny game's limb. A
            gameplay heating threshold would be an authored boundary, not a
            literal edge visible here.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-1">
        02.1 / Three worlds in the same flight scene · retained observations
      </summary>
      <p>
        Read the whole scene before opening the enlarged views: the ship, the
        world's mass, its atmosphere and its relation to the other planets. The
        dim dashed gravity boundary reuses the game's existing language. Earth
        borrows Venus's current mass only as a comparison fixture; no Earth
        gravity tuning is selected.
      </p>
      {concepts.map((concept) => (
        <article>
          <h4>{concept.title}</h4>
          <p>{concept.question}</p>
          <img
            src={`${root}/${concept.id}-scene.jpg`}
            alt={`${concept.title}: retained flight-scale comparison with Sun, Mercury, Venus and Wasp`}
            width="1000"
            height="480"
          />
          <p class="caption">
            Static closure capture · 15 November 2026, 1280px browser. Captured
            from the existing comparison before retiring its component; not a
            recording of the earlier review.
          </p>
          <p class="caption">Initial uncertainty · {concept.limit}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
      <details>
        <summary>
          Enlarged construction views / not the flight-scale verdict
        </summary>
        <div class="details-grid">
          {concepts.map((concept) => (
            <figure>
              <img
                src={`${root}/${concept.id}.png`}
                alt={`${concept.title}, enlarged concept artwork`}
                width="280"
                height="280"
              />
              <figcaption>{concept.title}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Comparison conditions and limits</summary>
        <p>
          Static 1000 × 480 stage at 0.7 CSS px/u. Practice G7 Sun, seed 71,
          time zero; Mercury r27u at orbit 258u and angle −0.6; Venus
          r40.327526u at 482.134715u and angle 0.42. Earth fixture r44u at Sun
          offset (700, 0)u; Wasp at Earth offset (77, 35)u, heading −π/2,
          production build scale. Camera centre is Sun offset (330, 0)u. The
          1254px artworks are normalized by their opaque horizontal midlines (A:
          1158px; B: 1216px; C: 1165px); atmospheric falloff remains outside the
          approximately 61.6px body. Materials held at phase zero; no simulation
          steps. Earth gravity fixture uses Venus mass 2844.850581, yielding the
          existing law's roughly 238.53u reach. Placement, size and mass do not
          select a Practice roster change.
        </p>
        <p>
          The comparison uses shared FlightLayers, production body painters,
          ship drawing, Sun fields and World HUD. Earth is original generated
          concept artwork with authored left illumination, drawn on World
          without an additional light mask. Other planets retain production
          World/LFX lighting. Images were separately authored, not a controlled
          single-variable material test; shadow, geography and colour differ.
          C’s settlement lights form long coastal traces, so an outline-like
          reading remains a visible limitation rather than a selected settlement
          pattern. The concepts do not establish rotation, global geography,
          generation reliability, flight feel or a production renderer.
          <a href={`${root}/prompts.json`}>Exact prompts and provenance</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <h4 id="02-1-findings">
        Findings / recognition, life and visible habitation
      </h4>
      <p>
        Design review · 15 November 2026. Familiar continents likely carry
        Earth's recognition, yet the exposed-geography treatment also leaves the
        world feeling uninhabited. Recognition and a sense of habitation are
        distinct achievements in these examples; this does not rule out
        recognizable continents.
      </p>
      <p>
        Weather makes the world feel more alive and more plausibly inhabited by
        something than the treatment with less weather. This is a retained
        observation. This finding preserves the contribution of weather, not
        every cloud shape, colour or lighting choice in 02.1 B, and it does not
        by itself establish technological civilization.
      </p>
      <p>
        The small settlement lights in 02.1 C vanish at flight scale. Realistic
        or even semi-realistic light sizes cannot carry recognition at this
        scale; visible habitation needs deliberate exaggeration if light is its
        cue. The amount, distribution and drawing language of that exaggeration
        remain open. These findings concern the demonstrated static scenes, not
        all Earth-like worlds or motion.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-2">
        02.2 / Visible detail did not establish belonging
      </summary>
      <p>
        Design review · 15 November 2026. The detailed Earth and Earth-like
        images read as rendered planet images, not as objects belonging to Void
        Drift. Enlarging the settlement lights exposed this failure of the
        visual approach. Recognizable geography, weather and readable habitation
        cues did not establish a drawing language that felt part of the game.
        The light proposals are withdrawn.
      </p>
      <p>
        Weather's contribution to a sense of life remains a useful finding, as
        does the need to exaggerate small cues if they are to be legible.
        Neither finding accepts the detailed globe as the basis for further
        refinement. The preceding comparison remains evidence for those
        observations, not a selected appearance. This round does not establish
        that flat shapes, fewer details, procedural generation or another
        rendering technique would solve the mismatch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Return to the unresolved visual-language question: what makes this world
        belong beside the game's ship, Sun and planets? The next proposal needs
        a different whole-scene drawing idea whose material and light can be
        judged in that company. Settlement treatment and atmospheric heating
        remain open while that direction is reconsidered.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-3">02.3 / Depth survived; the drawing language did not</h3>
    <p>
      Design review · 15 November 2026. Cloud coverage appearing above the
      ground is promising. Both drawings otherwise read as vector-style icons.
      That spatial relationship is retained as a finding; neither drawing is a
      visual direction. The drawings are withdrawn.
    </p>
    <h3 id="02-4">02.4 / Crossing the atmospheric edge</h3>
    <p>
      The whole-world question was too broad to diagnose through another
      complete planet image. This round narrows the inquiry to what a pilot sees
      before entry, during a close pass and on leaving. Weather depth and
      atmospheric encounter cues must be considered together; their visual
      boundaries may imply different things.
    </p>
    <p>
      Design review · 15 November 2026. The encounter example was rejected for
      misusing the Motion instrument: it did not follow the design system, the
      instrument specification or its intended use. The example and its
      dedicated code are withdrawn. It establishes no finding about atmospheric
      boundaries or heating cues. The encounter question remains unanswered; the
      broader Earth recognition and habitation questions also remain open.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / Readiness not reached</h2>
    <p>
      No visual direction was accepted. Technical feasibility checks did not
      establish visual belonging or the usefulness of the experiments.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>04 / No implementation scope</h2>
    <p>
      The study ended during exploration. No production change is authorized by
      its findings. The separate diagnosis does not reopen this study.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
img {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

summary {
  cursor: pointer;
  margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
}
article {
  margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
.references {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--spacing-lg);
}
.references img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 220px;
  object-fit: contain;
}

.details-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--spacing-lg);
}
figure {
  margin: 0;
}
figcaption,
.caption {
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
figcaption {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
